\title{Nonlinear one-sided PCA with the R package \pkg{ospca}}
\author{}
\date{\pkg{ospca} version \Sexpr{packageVersion("ospca")}}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

<<setup, include = FALSE>>=
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", size = "small",
                      fig.width = 8, fig.height = 4, out.width = "\\textwidth",
                      fig.align = "center", fig.pos = "!htb")
options(ospca.threads = 2, width = 80)
@

\section{Introduction}

In the linear one-sided PCA of \code{ospca()} (see
\code{vignette("ospca", package = "ospca")}), a direction $u$ describes the
half-line $\{r u: r > 0\}$. The nonlinear one-sided PCA of \code{nlospca()}
replaces this half-line by its image under the center-outward quantile function
$Q_\pm$ of the distribution of $X$ (Hallin et al., 2021). The function $Q_\pm$
is the gradient of a convex function that pushes the spherical uniform
distribution on the open unit ball forward to the distribution of $X$. It maps
the origin to the center, a multivariate median, and the sphere of radius $r$
to the quantile contour of order $r$. The image of the half-line,
$r \mapsto Q_\pm(r u)$, $0 < r < 1$, is the so-called sign curve of $u$. Then
\begin{itemize}
\item the principal directions maximize the expected squared length of the sign
  curves, greedily over the directions orthogonal to the previous ones and their
  antipodes, as in the linear case;
\item the principal curves are the sign curves of the principal directions;
\item the principal components are curvilinear projections onto these curves.
\end{itemize}

In the sample, the $n$ observations are optimally assigned to a regular grid in
the unit ball, which consists of $n_S$ directions times $n_R$ radii
$j / (n_R + 1)$, $j = 1, \ldots, n_R$, and $n_0$ copies of the origin, with
$n = n_R n_S + n_0$. This gives the empirical center-outward quantile function
$Q_\pm^{(n)}$ at the grid points, which is extended to the whole ball by a
cyclically monotone interpolation $\Qbar$. As the empirical sign curves are
rough, they are regularized by a Tikhonov filter before their lengths are
computed. In this vignette we illustrate the method, its helper functions and
its methods on a simple bivariate data set.

\section{Data}

The observations lie around a parabola, so that the data have two arms of
different length:

<<data>>=
set.seed(1)
x <- rnorm(400)
X <- cbind(x1 = x, x2 = x^2 + rnorm(400, sd = 0.5))
@

\section{Fitting the model}

<<fit>>=
library(ospca)
fit <- nlospca(X)
fit
@

The grid is chosen automatically, with $n_R$ close to $\sqrt{n} / 2$; here
$400 = 10 \cdot 39 + 10$. It can also be set by the arguments \code{nR} and
\code{nS}. By default, all $2d = 4$ directions are kept. The summary also gives
the settings and the diagnostics of the direction search:

<<summary, size = "scriptsize">>=
summary(fit)
@

A positive margin of the potentials means that the optimal assignment is
strictly cyclically monotone, so that the interpolation is exact at the grid
points. In the direction diagnostics, \code{gap} is the relative gap between the
criterion of a direction and that of the best candidate more than 45 degrees
away, and \code{tie} flags a gap below \code{tol\_tie}, in which case the
maximizer is effectively not unique.

Note that the principal directions are unit vectors in the unit ball, the space
of the center-outward signs, and not in the space of the data:

<<directions>>=
directions(fit)
@

The second direction is orthogonal to the first, and the third and the fourth
are the antipodes of the first two.

\section{Principal curves}

\code{plot()} draws the data together with the principal curves, which start at
the center (black dot); see Figure~\ref{fig:plot}. The first curve follows the
longer left arm, the second one the right arm, and the last two run into the
bottom and the left of the central part of the data.

<<plot, fig.width = 4.5, fig.height = 4.2, out.width = "0.55\\textwidth", fig.cap = "Data with the four principal curves, which start at the center (black dot).">>=
plot(fit)
@

The curves are part of the fit (\code{fit\$curves}), and \code{nlos\_curve()}
evaluates the regularized sign curve of any direction, starting at the center.
For the direction pointing straight up, which lies between the first two
principal directions, the curve runs into the right arm:

<<curve>>=
C_up <- nlos_curve(c(0, 1), fit)
dim(C_up)
tail(C_up, 3)
@

\section{The criterion}

The criterion is the average, over the radii $r_j$, of the squared polygonal
length of the regularized sign curve from the center to $r_j u$, where a
proportion \code{delta} of the outer radii is left out. \code{nlos\_objective()}
evaluates it for given directions, one per row. For the fitted directions, it
reproduces the criterion values of the fit:

<<objective>>=
nlos_objective(t(directions(fit)), fit)
fit$objective
@

Figure~\ref{fig:objective-plot} shows the criterion as a function of the angle
of the direction, with the four principal directions marked. The second
direction is the best one orthogonal to the first (or the antipode of the
first), and so on.

<<objective-plot, fig.height = 3.6, fig.cap = "Criterion as a function of the angle of the direction, with the four principal directions.">>=
angle <- function(u) (atan2(u[[2]], u[[1]]) * 180 / pi) %% 360
theta <- seq(0, 359.5, by = 0.5)
U <- cbind(cos(theta * pi / 180), sin(theta * pi / 180))
M <- nlos_objective(U, fit)
plot(theta, M, type = "l", xlab = "angle of u (degrees)", ylab = "criterion",
     ylim = c(0, 1.1 * max(M)))
points(apply(directions(fit), 2, angle), fit$objective, pch = 19)
text(apply(directions(fit), 2, angle), fit$objective, 1:4, pos = 3)
@

Note that the empirical criterion is rough, with narrow spikes. Therefore, the
directions are found by a search over a dense grid of \code{mS} directions and
not by a gradient method. By default, \code{mS} is the smallest multiple of
\code{nS} that is at least $n$, here \Sexpr{fit$settings$mS}, so that the grid
contains the directions of the target grid. A coarser grid, e.g., one with
$4 n_S$ directions, misses the narrow spike at the first direction. In this
example, however, the reconstruction error with the first direction (see
Section~\ref{sec:reconstruction}) hardly changes:

<<mS>>=
fit_coarse <- nlospca(X, mS = 4 * fit$settings$nS)
fit_coarse$objective
c(reconstruction_mse(X, reconstruct(fit, n_components = 1)),
  reconstruction_mse(X, reconstruct(fit_coarse, n_components = 1)))
@

\section{Quantile and distribution functions}

\code{nlos\_quantile()} evaluates the interpolated empirical quantile function
$\Qbar$ at points of the unit ball, and \code{nlos\_distribution()} evaluates
the empirical center-outward distribution function, which maps a point of the
data space to a point of the grid. The norm of this grid point is the
center-outward rank of the observation, divided by $n_R + 1$. With the default
\code{kappa = 0}, the interpolation is piecewise constant and returns the
observations at their own grid points:

<<quantile>>=
G <- nlos_distribution(fit, X[1:4, ])
G
sqrt(rowSums(G^2)) * (fit$settings$nR + 1)    # center-outward ranks
nlos_quantile(fit, G)
X[1:4, ]
@

The images of circles of radius $r$ are the empirical quantile contours of order
$r$ (Figure~\ref{fig:contours}). Note that they are closed curves around the
center and therefore cross the region between the two arms, where there are no
observations.

<<contours, fig.width = 4.5, fig.height = 4.2, out.width = "0.55\\textwidth", fig.cap = "Empirical quantile contours of orders 0.25, 0.5 and 0.75.">>=
plot(X, pch = 16, col = "grey70", asp = 1)
circle <- cbind(cos(seq(0, 2 * pi, length.out = 200)),
                sin(seq(0, 2 * pi, length.out = 200)))
for (r in c(0.25, 0.5, 0.75)) lines(nlos_quantile(fit, r * circle), col = "navy")
points(fit$center[1], fit$center[2], pch = 19)
@

\section{Scores of new observations}

The scores are the lengths of the curvilinear projections onto the principal
curves:

<<scores>>=
head(scores(fit))
@

\code{predict()} gives the scores of new observations. These are mapped to the
grid by the cyclically monotone extension of the empirical distribution function
and are then projected in the same way. A point on the left arm has its largest
score for the first component, a point on the right arm for the second one, and
a point below the center for the third one:

<<predict>>=
Xnew <- rbind(c(-2, 4), c(2, 4), c(0, -0.5))
predict(fit, Xnew)
@

Note that the point on the right arm also has a sizable score for the first
component, as the beginnings of the first two curves both rise from the center.

\section{Reconstruction}\label{sec:reconstruction}

\code{reconstruct()} projects the observations onto the nonlinear manifold given
by the first $k$ principal curves, and \code{reconstruction\_mse()} gives the
mean squared reconstruction error. With \code{smooth = FALSE} the unsmoothed
curves are used, and with all $2d$ directions and \code{kappa = 0} the
reconstruction is then exact, apart from the observations assigned to the
origin, which are replaced by their mean.

<<reconstruct>>=
sapply(1:4, function(k) reconstruction_mse(X, reconstruct(fit, n_components = k)))
sapply(1:4, function(k)
  reconstruction_mse(X, reconstruct(fit, n_components = k, smooth = FALSE)))
@

\code{screeplot()} shows the criterion values (bars) together with the
reconstruction errors (line), see Figure~\ref{fig:screeplot}, and
\code{fitted()} and \code{residuals()} give the reconstructions and the data
minus their reconstructions:

<<screeplot, fig.width = 4.5, fig.height = 4, out.width = "0.55\\textwidth", fig.cap = "Criterion values (bars) and reconstruction errors (line).">>=
screeplot(fit)
@

<<fitted>>=
head(fitted(fit, n_components = 2))
head(residuals(fit, n_components = 2))
@

\section{Tuning parameters}

The Tikhonov parameter \code{eps} regularizes the sign curves whose lengths
define the criterion. Without regularization (\code{eps = 0}), the empirical
sign curves zigzag between the observations, and the criterion then rewards
directions with long zigzags. In our example, the first curve then jumps
between the two arms (Figure~\ref{fig:eps}).

<<eps, fig.cap = "Principal curves without regularization (left) and with the default regularization (right).">>=
fit0 <- nlospca(X, eps = 0)
par(mfrow = c(1, 2))
plot(fit0, main = "eps = 0")
plot(fit, main = "eps = 0.5 (default)")
@

The other main arguments are:
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{eps\_curves}, the regularization of the returned curves and of the
  projections, by default equal to \code{eps};
\item \code{paths}, which decides whether the observation's own sign curve is
  regularized in the same way (\code{"smooth"}, the default) or not
  (\code{"raw"});
\item \code{delta}, the proportion of the outer radii left out of the criterion;
\item \code{kappa}, the Yosida regularization of the interpolation, with
  \code{0} for the piecewise constant interpolation;
\item \code{mR} and \code{mS}, the number of radii of the curves and the number
  of directions searched;
\item \code{ot\_method}, the optimal transport algorithm of the package
  \pkg{transport};
\item \code{nthreads}, the number of threads of the compiled code, which can
  also be set by \code{options(ospca.threads = )}. In this vignette two threads
  are used.
\end{itemize}

